:null,"_gp_fund_growth":null,"_score_fund_growth":0.0,"_gate_shrhldr_yield":0.020793248865788903,"_gp_shrhldr_yield":true,"_score_shrhldr_yield":34.2,"_gate_share_shrink":0.060062635360551786,"_gp_share_shrink":false,"_score_share_shrink":0.0,"_gate_sbc_dilution":0.10016617936006492,"_gp_sbc_dilution":false,"_score_sbc_dilution":0.0,"_gate_insider_own":0.0039399997,"_gp_insider_own":false,"_score_insider_own":2.6,"_gate_insider_buying":0.6,"_gp_insider_buying":true,"_score_insider_buying":60.0,"pf":[],"description_full":"Rexford Industrial Realty, Inc. creates value by investing in, operating and repositioning industrial properties throughout infill Southern California, the world's fourth largest industrial market and consistently the highest-demand with lowest-supply major market in the nation over the long term. The Company's highly differentiated strategy enables internal and external growth opportunities through its proprietary value creation and asset management capabilities. As of June 30, 2026, Rexford Industrial's high-quality, irreplaceable portfolio comprised 409 properties with approximately 49.9 million rentable square feet occupied by a stable and diverse tenant base. Rexford Industrial Realty, Inc. was incorporated on January 18th, 2013, and is based in Los Angeles, United States.","ceo_bio":"Ms. Laura Elizabeth Clark | CEO & Director | Age 45 | Compensation: $2.1M (FY2025)","culture_narrative":"Employs approximately 256 people. Revenue per employee of $3.9M ranks in the above the sector median among Real Estate peers, with revenue growing 28%/yr across its filed history. Each employee generates $815K of free cash flow annually. Stock-based compensation of $392K per employee reflects an ownership culture. CEO compensation is 1\u00d7 revenue per employee (modest relative to workforce productivity). Governance score flags elevated compensation governance risk (8/10).","financial_summary":["Rexford Industrial Realty, Inc. is a mid-cap real estate business with a $8.2B market cap on $1.0B of annual revenue.","The top line has compounded at +17% over the past three years and +27% annually across the full decade, the kind of sustained demand persistence that marks a compounder.","The business throws off $209M in free cash flow \u2014 roughly 21 cents on every revenue dollar, which is the owner-earnings figure that actually belongs to shareholders, and cash conversion sits at 2.56\u00d7 so those profits are backed by real cash.","On the balance sheet, debt is heavy at 9.8\u00d7 net debt/EBITDA, and leverage like that is what turns a bad year into a permanent loss, and thin 1.2\u00d7 interest coverage leaves no room for a bad year.","Shareholder yield of 2.1% reflects a genuine commitment to returning capital rather than empire-building, and stock-based comp at 10% of revenue is silent dilution an owner has to account for, and insider ownership is negligible, so management has little personal stake in the long-term outcome."],"sector_headwinds":[{"text":"Altman Z in the distress zone (1.4) \u2014 the kind of balance-sheet risk that can make a loss permanent","sev":"red","cat":"quality"},{"text":"Payout ratio of 185% leaves essentially no coverage if earnings wobble","sev":"red","cat":"dividend"},{"text":"Above-average short interest at 7.8% of float","sev":"amber","cat":"sentiment"},{"text":"Interest-rate sensitivity and tenant-credit risk \u2014 cap rates and refinancing costs move faster than the 10-year leases can absorb.","sev":"amber","cat":"thesis_risk"},{"text":"Remote work structural vacancy \u2014 the post-pandemic shift to hybrid work has permanently impaired demand for urban office space across most markets.","sev":"amber","cat":"thesis_risk"},{"text":"Climate and insurance cost escalation \u2014 rising premiums and coverage withdrawals in flood/fire zones are repricing assets that the market once treated as perpetuities.","sev":"amber","cat":"thesis_risk"}],"sector_tailwinds":["Growing faster than most Real E